    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making \"cold.\" A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it passes through a compressor that squeezes it, making it hot. That heat escapes through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge. Then the refrigerant expands, becoming very cold. As it flows through pipes inside the fridge, it absorbs heat from your food and air. The refrigerant carries that heat back outside, and the cycle repeats. A thermostat watches the temperature and turns the compressor on or off to keep things just right. Insulation in the walls helps trap the cold inside, so your milk and leftovers stay chilled."
  }
